WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.180
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: The report you have in front
of you sets out the latest financial performance

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:18.071
data for the commissioning system, and our
forecast for the remainder of the year. It

00:00:18.071 --> 00:00:21.460
is hot off the presses, so I do not on this
occasion have to do that rather difficult

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.630
task of describing the month-five numbers
with you looking at the month-four numbers

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:31.600
in front of you, so you have the numbers that
I have. As the paper notes, probably more

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.030
importantly perhaps than the numbers themselves,
we are just in the middle of an in-depth stock-take

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:41.980
with regional and local teams to achieve really
three things. Firstly, to test the robustness

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.140
of what is essentially a balanced forecast
position for the commissioning system as a

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:51.760
whole that you have in front of you. Secondly,
to understand the interventions already underway

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.650
across the country to manage what is clearly
a higher level of risk than we have seen in

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.320
previous years. Thirdly, to agree any further
actions that we need to take to ensure that

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:07.470
we sustain our 100% track record, so far at
least, of living within our means.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:11.440
The report highlights the reasons why this
is more difficult this year. Essentially,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.330
we have had to increase the level of commissioner
efficiencies by nearly 50% overall, and more

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:22.330
than that in the CCG sector, in order to create
the headroom for the 1% investment and risk

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.850
reserve that we are holding this year.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.450
Our success in managing the risks that I have
talked about, and equally NHS Improvement’s

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.409
success in minimising the deficit in the provider
sector, is therefore essential if we are going

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:40.910
to secure that financial balance and, equally
importantly, maximise the proportion of the

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:44.979
reserve that I have just talked about which
can be invested locally in support of our

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:50.580
Five Year Forward View priorities rather than
being released to offset overspends.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.240
It is also important for another reason, which
Simon mentioned already, which is to set the

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.320
right starting point for the operating plans
that we discussed earlier in this meeting,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.240
which require both the commissioning sector
and the provider sector, together but also

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.080
individually, to be in financial balance next
year. Clearly to the extent that we do not

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.450
achieve that this year, through the measures
that we are talking about, the task for next

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.310
year becomes even more significant.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:23.150
Perhaps just one parenthesis. Given the ongoing
speculation in various quarters, it is perhaps

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.400
just worth reminding ourselves of Simon’s
comment back in June, which is still very

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.510
much valid: that there is no realistic prospect
of some external solution coming for all these

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:38.560
problems, external funding or whatever, in
the next few months or indeed the next few

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.340
years. So all of us – CCGs, NHS England
and the provider community – need to be

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.080
planning and then delivering the financial
balance within the resources that we have

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.440
available now, rather than waiting for some
deus ex machina solution that simply will

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:59.790
not be forthcoming over the next few months.
If we defer taking the robust action that

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.510
we need to take now in order to see whether
that might possibly happen, it will be too

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:10.310
late by the time we discover that that is
not a realistic prospect. That is the task

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:11.680
in hand.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.380
Appendix A in the report you have in front
of you shows you how things are going so far.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.590
We are, as I say, forecasting what is effectively
a break-even position on the bottom line;

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:27.050
a slight overspend of £24 million, less than
0.1%, which is frankly well within the roundings

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:32.629
on a £106&nbsp;billion budget at this stage of
the year. You can also see that the challenges

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:37.550
which I have just described are already evident
in the delivery of the CCG sector, with year-to-date

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:43.769
overspends in just over a third of the CCG
population, totalling £159 million.

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:48.069
If you cast a quick eye across to Appendix
D, you can see that that correlates strongly

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:52.210
with the shortfalls in the delivery of commissioner
efficiencies. However, again I would just

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:57.360
remind you that that is against a much higher
level of ambition than in previous years.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.980
The year-to-date performance shown in Appendix
D is running at 85% of plan amongst CCGs,

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:08.840
but it still represents a 23% increase over
the equivalent period last year.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:13.209
It is also noticeable that, by way of contrast
with previous years, the pressure amongst

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:18.350
CCGs is being felt across the whole country,
with CCGs in the North in particular now having

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:23.020
to adjust to budgets which are closer to their
target allocations.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:27.840
As usual, spending on central budgets has
got off to a relatively slow start, so the

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.710
commissioning overspends you can see in the
year-to-date position have been partially

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:36.760
offset at least by slippage in NHS England
running and programme costs. The big question

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:42.111
for us is how much of the commissioning position
can be recovered by the end of the year. The

00:04:42.111 --> 00:04:46.160
current forecast, as you can see, shows that
in most regions CCGs are expecting to see

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.830
significant improvements in financial performance
over the coming months. If they achieve that,

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:57.090
we can offset the residual overspend by a
variety of one-off factors shown in the forecast,

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:01.170
particularly relating to rates rebates, and
by holding back spending against our central

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:05.440
budgets. Even allowing for the oddities of
treasury accounting, which are reflected in

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.229
the adjustments towards the bottom of the
table, we have a clear line of sight to a

00:05:09.229 --> 00:05:11.240
balanced position.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:16.280
Paragraphs 15 and 16 of my report set out
the risks to that position, mostly relating

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.550
to the CCG sector, and this is the focus of
our so-called ‘deep‑dive review’. It

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:26.280
would be fair to say that the work to date
on that review indicates that, while recovery

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:31.880
for actions already underway will ensure that
not all of the £444 million of commissioning

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:37.770
risk will crystallise, a substantial proportion
of it probably will. As well as trying to

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.550
steer that number down to the lowest possible
level by supporting CCGs to take the required

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:47.630
action to correct that position, we are also
redoubling our efforts to find additional

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:53.449
mitigations amongst our central budgets.
We remain absolutely committed to delivering

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:57.480
a balanced position across the commissioning
sector; there is no question about that being

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:03.050
our intent and our resolve. I very much expect
that, when I come back to you in a couple

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.900
of months’ time at our next meeting, I will
be able to describe how the combined impact

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:12.169
of all of those actions – the corrective
actions in the CCG sector, the controls that

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:16.710
we have tightly in place around expenditure
on our direct commissioning budgets, and the

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:23.759
additional mitigations in central budgets
– will enable me to show you how we are

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:29.820
going to deliver on the commitment that we
have to deliver a balanced bottom line. I

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.380
can assure you right now: there is no stone
being left unturned in the pursuit of that

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.380
goal.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.900
Meanwhile, as I note in my report, our colleagues
in NHS Improvement are working flat out with

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:45.820
trusts around the country to ensure that the
actions we signalled back in July in the so‑called

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.840
‘reset document’ have the maximum possible
impact in securing – and, where possible,

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:55.300
improving on – the £580 million deficit
plan that they have agreed with providers,

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:01.889
and heading down towards the £250 million
achievement which they have set out for themselves.

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:06.880
Again, that process is not yet complete, and
work continues both for the first cohort of

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:11.100
providers put into special measures, and with
a wider group of providers with cost growth

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:15.370
issues to address, so we will have to wait
a few weeks before we know where that is going

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.970
to land.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:21.180
All in all, a year which was always going
to be exceptionally challenging is living

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.940
up to our expectations, at least in that respect.
Across the commissioning sector, we can draw

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.030
on the strengths of the financial management
approach that we have evolved over our first

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:34.970
three years of operation, but there is still
a hell of a lot to do, and that stuff has

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.569
got to be done in combination with the work
that we have been talking about already this

00:07:38.569 --> 00:07:44.449
morning on STPs and landing the longer-term
plans, and on gearing up for the operating

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:49.030
plan process, which, again, we discussed.
Major activity around the country to secure

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:54.280
the financial position, alongside the longer-term
work that we have been discussing already.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:59.300
On which happy note, I will pause for questions
or, even better, contributions, monetary or

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.500
otherwise, from colleagues around the table.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:10.039
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for your
usual clarity and the lack of sunny uplands

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:17.080
which always characterises your report.
David Roberts: I just think the Board should

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:22.819
be aware that the Commissioning Committee
went through the numbers with Paul in some

00:08:22.819 --> 00:08:28.169
depth, and would support everything that he
said. I think the point that is self-evident

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:34.250
is that the risk is probably as high as we
have seen it, and therefore there is a lot

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:42.159
of work needed to be done to reduce that risk
and mitigate it. However, Paul and the team

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:52.259
have a really good grip on the detail and
on the drivers of cost and how they manage

00:08:52.259 --> 00:08:58.889
themselves through into the numbers, and therefore
I think we can be reassured that all the right

00:08:58.889 --> 00:09:02.049
things are being done.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I would agree with that.

00:09:02.049 --> 00:09:08.139
I think the other pillar is the relationship
with NHS Improvement, which is just so different

00:09:08.139 --> 00:09:14.300
from that which we had only one year ago,
and the close working relationship which I

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:18.889
know exists across our organisations is going
to be absolutely critical to delivering what

00:09:18.889 --> 00:09:26.040
we need to deliver. I think that continues
to be work in progress, to continue to build

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.760
on what we have achieved already.

